# Codex compatibility

The shared rules and skills use Claude Code terminology. Preserve their intent
and translate host-specific primitives instead of invoking nonexistent Claude
tools or literal Claude model IDs.

- `Opus` or `opus` maps to `gpt-5.6-sol`: complex planning, incident
  orchestration, ambiguous work, and quality-first tasks.
- `Sonnet` or `sonnet` maps to `gpt-5.6-terra`: implementation, review, and
  everyday agentic work.
- `Haiku` or `haiku` maps to `gpt-5.6-luna`: mechanical, repetitive,
  high-volume, and latency-sensitive work.
- The mappings preserve workload roles; they are not claims of exact model
  equivalence. Keep the selected Codex reasoning effort unless the task needs a
  deliberate change.
- Translate Claude's `Agent` operation to Codex subagent delegation. Translate
  `Explore` to Codex's `explorer`, general implementation to `worker`, and
  general-purpose work to `default` when no narrower custom agent fits.
- Translate `WebSearch` and `WebFetch` to Codex web access, and translate
  `AskUserQuestion` to Codex's available user-input mechanism.
- When a shared skill names a Claude-only mode, hook, status line, transcript,
  or command, use the closest Codex-native feature. If no equivalent exists,
  preserve the workflow and report the unsupported host-specific step instead
  of pretending it ran.
- Caveman and Ponytail refer to their Codex plugins and bundled skills. Prefer
  their Codex-native hooks and agents when the installed plugin exposes them.
- Codex configuration is managed by `{{ playbook_dir }}/tasks/codex.yml` and
  `{{ playbook_dir }}/.codex/`; Claude configuration remains managed under
  `{{ playbook_dir }}/.claude/`.

